Superficial solar erythema (sunburn) from prolonged UV exposure without protection to shoulders
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Confluent blanching erythema, Demarcation by clothing lines, Prolonged unprotected UV exposure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-038
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning pain and generalized skin hypersensitivity following excessive UV exposure, requiring assessment for systemic heat illness and deep thermal injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted broad areas of intensely painful, tight skin following severe solar exposure. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to differentiate simple first-degree sunburn from fluid-depleting second-degree blistering, and to definitively exclude concurrent systemic heat exhaustion/sunstroke prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was out in the sun all day without a shirt, and now my shoulders are radiating heat and the pain is unbearable.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of prolonged direct exposure to ultraviolet radiation without adequate chemical or physical barrier protection earlier today. This resulted in a delayed onset of pain peaking tonight. The patient reports diffuse tenderness and extreme hypersensitivity to light clothing friction or hot water. There is a subjective sensation of heat radiating physically from the affected skin surface. Patient denies severe nausea, confusion, intractable vomiting, or syncope.
Functional Status: Fully independent; pacing and unable to lie down comfortably due to posterior torso pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High-risk UV exposure behavior requiring preventative education.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/72 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate discomfort, visibly avoiding contact with clothing or furniture on the upper torso.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals broad, confluent areas of intense blanching erythema over the shoulders and upper back. The erythema is sharply demarcated by clothing lines. There is a complete absence of clear, fluid-filled vesicles or bullae, confirming a purely first-degree depth. The skin appears dry and tight without raw, weeping dermal layers. Mild localized, non-pitting edema is present in the highly exposed areas. Systemic vital signs are normal, lacking features of severe sunstroke or profound dehydration.
Environmental Observations: No immediate indoor hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Applied over-the-counter aloe gel with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial solar erythema (first-degree sunburn) to shoulders - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Partial-thickness (second-degree) thermal burn - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of tense, fluid-filled blisters or raw, weeping dermis.

Heat exhaustion/Sunstroke - Ruled out due to: Normal mentation, stable blood pressure, lack of severe tachycardia, and absence of systemic vomiting or profound lethargy.

Phototoxic drug eruption - Ruled out due to: Absence of newly initiated phototoxic medications (e.g., Doxycycline) and clear clinical correlation with purely unprotected solar exposure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Widespread solar erythema requires careful evaluation to rule out concurrent systemic heat illness and to accurately assess burn depth for fluid management. Bedside evaluation confidently confirmed an isolated first-degree thermal injury without systemic cardiovascular compromise or severe dehydration. Urgent after-hours visitation was essential to clear the patient of dangerous heatstroke, provide immediate high-dose systemic analgesia, and initiate aggressive topical barrier cooling, safely avoiding a highly uncomfortable and unnecessary emergency department presentation overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed scheduled oral NSAIDs (Ibuprofen) to directly inhibit the prostaglandin-mediated inflammatory cascade causing the severe erythema. Advised copious oral fluid hydration. Recommended topical application of unscented, thick emollients stored in the refrigerator.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a thick, refrigerated moisturizer to the burned skin 4 times a day. The chilled cream physically extracts trapped heat from the tight skin while restoring the depleted lipid barrier, providing massive, immediate relief from the burning sensation and preventing severe delayed peeling.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review not required unless severe, widespread blistering develops over the next 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if you develop severe dizziness upon standing, intractable vomiting, intense lethargy, confusion, or if massive fluid-filled blisters suddenly erupt over large areas of the chest or back.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Sunburn is an inflammatory process that can take up to 24-48 hours to fully peak; symptoms may slightly worsen before improving.
Clinical Course: The intense heat and pain will persist for 2 to 3 days. The damaged stratum corneum will then begin to dry out and aggressively peel over the following 5 to 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you stop urinating, feel dizzy enough to faint, or develop a high fever with uncontrollable shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Sunburn causes severe inflammation under the skin. Anti-inflammatory tablets like ibuprofen turn off the chemical signals causing this redness and pain, treating the burn from the inside out.
Actionable Steps: Take ibuprofen strictly with food. Drink at least 2 to 3 liters of water tomorrow. Take cool, short showers instead of hot baths. Always use SPF 50+ sunscreen outdoors.

